{
  "result": "The morning light is different. It doesn't demand anything from you. It just arrives, pale and gold, spilling over rooftops and through half-closed blinds, asking nothing more than to be noticed. I like to sit with it for a while, coffee steaming in my hands, watching the world wake up slowly. Outside, the street is quiet except for the rustle of a newspaper on a porch, the distant bark of a dog, the soft hum of a delivery truck making its first round. Inside my head, thoughts are still loose and unformed, not yet hardened into the sharp edges of to-do lists and deadlines. This is the best time to write, to think, to simply exist without performing. Ideas arrive like birds landing briefly on a fence: unexpected, fleeting, worth watching. Some stay. Most fly away. Both outcomes feel okay at this hour. We spend so much of our lives chasing noise, filling silence with podcasts, notifications, music, conversation, that we forget how spacious quiet can feel. It isn't empty. It's full of small things we usually miss. The way dust floats in a sunbeam. The way cold air feels on skin just before sunrise. The way your own breathing sounds when nothing competes with it. Maybe peace isn't a destination you travel toward, but a rhythm you remember. A return to something simple and slow, waiting patiently under all the hurry. And in that morning light, if you let it, you can remember too. It finds you again gently.",
